@ifnottex
@node Top
@top CMacs

CMacs extends Emacs with a deep GLib/GObject integration layer,
bringing GObject Introspection, a GLib-aware event loop, the Bacon
shell, Crispy C scripting, and a D-Bus service for cross-process
interaction.
@end ifnottex

@menu
* Overview::                    What CMacs adds to Emacs.
* GLib Event Loop::             How GMainContext integrates with pselect().
* GObject Bridge::              Wrapping GObjects in elisp.
* GObject Introspection::       Calling any GI-annotated library from elisp.
* D-Bus Service::               The org.cmacs.Editor1 interface.
* Bacon Shell::                 Interactive shell via vterm.
* cmacsgi Builtin::             Calling CMacs from the Bacon shell.
* CMacs API::                   Shared C library for scripting integration.
* Crispy C Scripting::          Inline C evaluation and config.
* Build Configuration::         Configure flags and dependencies.
@end menu

@c ===================================================================
@node Overview
@chapter Overview

CMacs is a customized build of Emacs that adds first-class GLib and
GObject support.  It is built from the Emacs source tree with
additional C modules under @file{cmacs/} and elisp under
@file{lisp/cmacs/}.

Key components:

@table @strong
@item GLib Event Loop
Integrates a GMainContext into Emacs's @code{pselect()}-based event
loop so that GLib sources (timers, D-Bus, sockets) fire naturally
within the Emacs event cycle---no threads, no races.

@item GObject Bridge
Wraps GObjects as elisp user-pointers with automatic reference
counting, property access, signal connection, and GClosure marshalling.

@item GObject Introspection
Loads typelibs at runtime and calls any GI-annotated function or method
from elisp, with full bidirectional type marshalling.

@item D-Bus Service
Exposes an @code{org.cmacs.Editor1} D-Bus interface so external
processes can call into CMacs for elisp evaluation, GI calls, and file
operations.

@item Bacon Shell
Runs the Bacon shell inside Emacs via vterm, with the @command{cmacsgi}
builtin providing 60+ subcommands for buffer management, text editing,
search/replace, window layout, project navigation, version control,
and package management---all without writing elisp.

@item Crispy C Scripting
Evaluates C code strings at runtime via the Crispy library, capturing
stdout/stderr output.
@end table

@noindent
Source layout:

@example
cmacs/
  glib/           GLib event loop, GObject bridge, GClosure, D-Bus,
                  eval dispatch
  gobject/        (symlinked into glib/)
  gi/             GObject Introspection bridge
  crispy/         Crispy C scripting
  bacon/          Bacon shell: C primitives, IPC, cmacs_bacon_main()
    modules/      cmacsgi builtin (transport abstraction + 60+ commands)
deps/bacon/       Bundled bacon source (built as libbacon-1.0.a)
lisp/cmacs/       Elisp integration layers
src/              Symlinks to cmacs/ files (for the Emacs build system)
@end example

@c ===================================================================
@node GLib Event Loop
@chapter GLib Event Loop Integration

@cindex GMainContext
@cindex event loop, GLib

CMacs creates a private @code{GMainContext} (separate from the GLib
default context that PGTK/GTK may own) and integrates it into Emacs's
@code{pselect()}-based event loop in @file{src/process.c}.

@section How It Works

@enumerate
@item
Before each @code{pselect()} call, @code{cmacs_glib_prepare()} queries
the GMainContext for its file descriptors and timeouts via
@code{g_main_context_query()}.  These fds are merged into Emacs's
@code{fd_set}s, and the timeout is reduced if GLib needs an earlier
wake-up.  Returns the highest GLib fd (for @code{nfds}).

@item
After @code{pselect()} returns, @code{cmacs_glib_dispatch()} maps the
results back to GLib's @code{GPollFD} structures, then calls
@code{g_main_context_check()} and @code{g_main_context_dispatch()} to
fire any ready GLib sources.

@item
If @code{pselect()} fails (@code{nfds < 0}), the context is released
without dispatching.
@end enumerate

@noindent
All GLib callbacks fire on the Emacs main thread.  No additional
threads are created.

@section Elisp Primitives

@table @code
@item cmacs-glib-context-p
Return non-nil if the GMainContext exists.

@item cmacs-glib-iteration &optional BLOCK
Run one iteration of the GLib main loop.

@item cmacs-glib-pending-p
Return non-nil if any GLib sources are pending.

@item cmacs-glib-timeout-add MS CALLBACK
Add a timeout source.  @var{CALLBACK} is called every @var{MS}
milliseconds.  If it returns nil, the timer is removed.  Returns a
source ID.

@item cmacs-glib-idle-add CALLBACK
Add an idle source.  Returns a source ID.

@item cmacs-glib-source-remove ID
Remove a GLib source by its ID.
@end table

@section Files

@table @file
@item cmacs/glib/cmacs-glib-loop.c
C implementation (hooks into @file{src/process.c}).
@item cmacs/glib/cmacs-glib-loop.h
Header with @code{cmacs_glib_prepare()} and @code{cmacs_glib_dispatch()}.
@end table

@c ===================================================================
@node GObject Bridge
@chapter GObject Bridge

@cindex GObject, elisp bridge
@cindex properties, GObject
@cindex signals, GObject

CMacs wraps GObjects as Emacs @code{user-ptr} values with automatic
reference counting.  When a wrapped GObject is garbage-collected,
@code{g_object_unref()} is called.

@section Elisp Primitives

@table @code
@item gobject-new TYPE &rest PROPERTIES
Construct a new GObject.  @var{TYPE} is the GType name string.
Properties are passed as alternating key-value pairs.

@item gobject-p OBJECT
Return non-nil if @var{OBJECT} is a wrapped GObject.

@item gobject-type-name OBJECT
Return the GType name of @var{OBJECT} as a string.

@item gobject-get OBJECT PROPERTY
Get a property value.  Marshals GValues to elisp automatically.

@item gobject-set OBJECT PROPERTY VALUE
Set a property value.  Marshals elisp to GValues automatically.

@item gobject-connect OBJECT SIGNAL CALLBACK
Connect @var{CALLBACK} (an elisp function) to @var{SIGNAL}.  Returns a
handler ID.  The callback receives signal arguments marshalled to elisp.

@item gobject-disconnect OBJECT HANDLER-ID
Disconnect a signal handler.

@item gobject-list-properties OBJECT
Return a list of property names.

@item gobject-list-signals OBJECT
Return a list of signal names.
@end table

@section GValue Marshalling

The bridge automatically converts between GValues and elisp:

@multitable @columnfractions .3 .3 .4
@headitem GType @tab Elisp Type @tab Notes
@item @code{G_TYPE_BOOLEAN} @tab @code{t}/@code{nil} @tab
@item @code{G_TYPE_INT} etc. @tab fixnum @tab All integer types
@item @code{G_TYPE_FLOAT/DOUBLE} @tab float @tab
@item @code{G_TYPE_STRING} @tab string @tab
@item @code{G_TYPE_ENUM/FLAGS} @tab fixnum @tab
@item @code{G_TYPE_OBJECT} @tab user-ptr @tab Wrapped GObject
@end multitable

@section GClosure Integration

Elisp functions can be used anywhere GLib expects a @code{GClosure}.
The @code{gobject-connect} primitive creates a closure that marshals
signal parameters to elisp, calls the function, and marshals any
return value back.

@section Files

@table @file
@item cmacs/gobject/cmacs-gobject.c
GObject wrapping, property access, signal connection.
@item cmacs/gobject/cmacs-gclosure.c
GClosure marshalling bridge.
@end table

@c ===================================================================
@node GObject Introspection
@chapter GObject Introspection

@cindex GI, GObject Introspection
@cindex typelib

CMacs can load GI typelibs at runtime and call any function or method
from any GI-annotated library (GLib, GTK, GStreamer, etc.) directly
from elisp.

@section Elisp Primitives

@table @code
@item gi-require NAMESPACE &optional VERSION
Load a typelib.  Example: @code{(gi-require "GLib" "2.0")}.

@item gi-call NAMESPACE FUNCTION &rest ARGS
Call a GI function.  Arguments are marshalled based on GI type info.
@example
(gi-require "GLib" "2.0")
(gi-call "GLib" "get_user_name")  @result{} "zach"
@end example

@item gi-method OBJECT METHOD &rest ARGS
Call a method on a GObject instance.

@item gi-enum NAMESPACE ENUM MEMBER
Resolve an enum value.
@example
(gi-enum "GLib" "ChecksumType" "sha256")  @result{} 2
@end example

@item gi-list-functions NAMESPACE
Return a list of all function names in @var{NAMESPACE}.

@item gi-function-info NAMESPACE FUNCTION
Return an alist describing the function signature: @code{name},
@code{args} (list of @code{(name . type)}), and @code{return-type}.
@end table

@section High-Level Elisp Interface

The @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-gi.el} layer provides convenience wrappers:

@table @code
@item cmacs-gi-require NAMESPACE &optional VERSION
Load a namespace and auto-generate elisp wrapper functions.  After
@code{(cmacs-gi-require "Gtk" "4.0")}, you can call
@code{(gtk-application-new "com.example" 0)}.

@item cmacs-gi-describe-function NAMESPACE FUNCTION
Interactive: display the function signature in a help buffer.
@end table

@section Files

@table @file
@item cmacs/gi/cmacs-gi.c
C bridge: typelib loading, argument marshalling, function invocation.
@item lisp/cmacs/cmacs-gi.el
Elisp convenience layer with namespace auto-generation.
@end table

@c ===================================================================
@node D-Bus Service
@chapter D-Bus Service

@cindex D-Bus
@cindex org.cmacs.Editor1

CMacs exposes a D-Bus service so that external processes can call into
CMacs.  The service uses GDBus and runs on the CMacs GMainContext, so
method handlers fire on the Emacs main thread.

@quotation Note
The embedded Bacon shell (@pxref{Bacon Shell}) uses a faster Unix
socketpair for IPC instead of D-Bus.  The D-Bus service remains
available for @emph{external} tools---scripts, other editors, desktop
automation---that want to communicate with CMacs from outside the
process.
@end quotation

@section Bus Name

Each CMacs instance owns a unique bus name:
@code{org.cmacs.Editor.Pid@var{PID}}, where @var{PID} is the Emacs
process ID.

@section Interface: org.cmacs.Editor1

Object path: @code{/org/cmacs/Editor}

@table @code
@item Eval (expression: s) @result{} (result: s)
Evaluate an elisp expression.  Returns the printed representation of
the result.  Errors are returned as D-Bus errors.

@item FindFile (path: s)
Open a file in CMacs.

@item Message (text: s)
Display a message in the CMacs minibuffer.

@item GiRequire (namespace: s, version: s) @result{} (success: b)
Load a GI namespace.

@item GiCall (namespace: s, function: s, args: as) @result{} (result: s)
Call a GI function.  Each argument string is read as a Lisp expression
(numbers pass through, bare words are auto-quoted as strings).

@item GiListFunctions (namespace: s) @result{} (functions: as)
List all functions in a loaded GI namespace.
@end table

@section Elisp Primitives

@table @code
@item cmacs-dbus-start
Start the D-Bus service.  Returns the bus name string.  Idempotent:
calling again returns the existing name.

@item cmacs-dbus-stop
Stop the D-Bus service and release the bus name.

@item cmacs-dbus-name
Return the current bus name, or nil if not running.
@end table

@section Testing with gdbus

@example
$ gdbus introspect --session \
    --dest org.cmacs.Editor.Pid12345 \
    --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor

$ gdbus call --session \
    --dest org.cmacs.Editor.Pid12345 \
    --object-path /org/cmacs/Editor \
    --method org.cmacs.Editor1.Eval "(+ 1 2)"
('3',)
@end example

@section Files

@table @file
@item cmacs/glib/cmacs-dbus-service.c
GDBus service implementation.
@item cmacs/glib/cmacs-dbus-service.h
Header.
@end table

@c ===================================================================
@node Bacon Shell
@chapter Bacon Shell

@cindex Bacon shell
@cindex vterm

Bacon is a Unix shell with integrated C scripting via Crispy.  CMacs
provides both a programmatic API (C primitives callable from elisp) and
an interactive shell via vterm.

@section Interactive Use: M-x bacon

The @code{bacon} command opens (or switches to) a Bacon shell in a
vterm buffer.  On launch it:

@enumerate
@item
Creates a Unix socketpair for IPC between the parent (Emacs) and child
(bacon shell).  The parent-side fd is watched on the CMacs GMainContext;
the child-side fd is passed via @env{CMACS_IPC_FD}.
@item
Starts the CMacs D-Bus service (@pxref{D-Bus Service}) and exports the
bus name via @env{CMACS_DBUS_NAME}, as a fallback for external tools.
@item
Sets @env{CMACS_MODULE_DIR} so the child can locate the @command{cmacsgi}
module directory.
@item
Creates a vterm buffer running @command{cmacs --bacon} (the same CMacs
binary, which enters shell mode when invoked with @option{--bacon}).
@end enumerate

@noindent
Because bacon is statically linked into CMacs, no external
@command{bacon} binary is required.  The @option{--bacon} flag causes
CMacs to skip all editor initialization and enter the Bacon shell REPL
directly.  @xref{cmacs --bacon}.

@section Programmatic C Primitives

These are available from elisp for scripted use (without vterm):

@table @code
@item bacon-start
Create and start a BaconShell instance.  Sources RC files and imports
bashrc aliases per the Bacon config.

@item bacon-stop
Destroy the BaconShell.

@item bacon-eval COMMAND
Execute a shell command string.  Returns @code{(EXIT-CODE . OUTPUT)}.
Uses tmpfile-based stdout/stderr capture to avoid deadlocks.  Redirects
stdin to @file{/dev/null} to prevent @code{tcsetpgrp} job control from
stealing the terminal foreground.

@item bacon-eval-c CODE
Execute a C code block via Crispy.  Returns @code{(EXIT-CODE . OUTPUT)}.

@item bacon-complete PREFIX
Get tab-completion candidates for @var{PREFIX}.

@item bacon-environment
Return the shell environment as an alist.

@item bacon-source FILE
Source a file in the shell.

@item bacon-alias &optional NAME VALUE
Get or set a shell alias.

@item bacon-running-p
Return non-nil if the shell backend is active.

@item bacon-get-prompt
Return the configured prompt format string.
@end table

@anchor{cmacs --bacon}
@section cmacs --bacon

When invoked as @command{cmacs --bacon}, CMacs enters shell mode:

@enumerate
@item
The @option{--bacon} flag is detected very early in @code{main()},
before any Emacs initialization.
@item
@code{cmacs_bacon_main()} strips the flag from argv, creates a
@code{BaconShell}, registers the @command{cmacsgi} builtin (using the
fd transport from @env{CMACS_IPC_FD}), sources @file{~/.baconrc}, and
enters the shell REPL.
@item
The function never returns---it calls @code{exit()} when the shell
terminates.
@end enumerate

@noindent
This mode is primarily used by @code{M-x bacon} (which launches
@command{cmacs --bacon} in vterm), but it can also be used standalone:

@example
$ cmacs --bacon
bacon$ echo "hello from bacon"
hello from bacon
bacon$ cmacsgi eval "(+ 1 2)"    # only works with IPC fd
@end example

@noindent
When run standalone (outside of @code{M-x bacon}), the @command{cmacsgi}
builtin falls back to D-Bus if @env{CMACS_IPC_FD} is not set.

@section Customization

@table @code
@item bacon-buffer-name
Name of the Bacon shell buffer (default @code{"*bacon*"}).
@item bacon-shell-program
Path to the bacon shell binary.  Defaults to the CMacs binary itself
(i.e.@: @code{invocation-name} in @code{invocation-directory}), which
is invoked with @option{--bacon}.
@end table

@section Elisp IPC Primitives

@table @code
@item bacon-ipc-start
Create a Unix socketpair for parent--child IPC.  Attaches the parent-side
fd to the CMacs GMainContext and returns the child-side fd number.
@item bacon-ipc-stop
Destroy the IPC handler and close the parent-side fd.
@end table

@section Files

@table @file
@item cmacs/bacon/cmacs-bacon.c
C primitives (bacon-start, bacon-eval, etc.) and @code{cmacs_bacon_main()}.
@item cmacs/bacon/cmacs-bacon-ipc.c
Parent-side socketpair IPC handler: reads requests from the child,
dispatches to the shared eval layer, writes responses.
@item cmacs/glib/cmacs-eval-dispatch.c
Transport-agnostic eval dispatch used by both the D-Bus service and
the socketpair IPC handler.
@item lisp/cmacs/cmacs-bacon.el
Elisp layer: socketpair setup, vterm integration.
@end table

@c ===================================================================
@node cmacsgi Builtin
@chapter cmacsgi: Calling CMacs from Bacon

@cindex cmacsgi

The @command{cmacsgi} command is a Bacon shell builtin that lets you
control CMacs from the command line.  It communicates via a Unix
socketpair (when launched from @code{M-x bacon}) or falls back to
D-Bus (for standalone use), providing 60+ subcommands covering buffer
management, text editing, search/replace, window layout, project
navigation, version control, and more---all without writing a single
line of elisp.

@section Overview

@example
cmacsgi COMMAND [ARGS...]
cmacsgi --help                 list all commands
cmacsgi help COMMAND           help for a specific command
@end example

@noindent
Output conventions:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Tabular data: tab-separated, one record per line, no headers.
Pipe to @command{cut}, @command{awk}, @command{grep}.
@item
Single values: plain text with trailing newline.
@item
Raw content: buffer text, diffs---no wrapping.
@item
Errors: @code{cmacsgi SUBCMD: message} to stderr, exit code 1.
@item
Side-effect operations (open, save, etc.): silent on success, exit 0.
@end itemize

@c ----- Buffer Commands -----------------------------------------------
@section Buffer Commands

@table @code
@item buf list [-l]
List open buffers.  Short format prints one buffer name per line.
With @option{-l}, prints tab-separated: name, file, modified flag,
size, major mode.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi buf list
*scratch*
*Messages*
main.c

bacon$ cmacsgi buf list -l
*scratch*	            *	0	lisp-interaction-mode
main.c	/home/zach/main.c		4096	c-mode
@end example

@item buf show [NAME]
Print buffer content to stdout.  If @var{NAME} is omitted, prints the
current buffer.

@item buf create NAME
Create an empty buffer.  Prints the buffer name.

@item buf kill NAME
Kill (close) a buffer.

@item buf save [NAME]
Save a buffer to its associated file.

@item buf save-as NAME PATH
Save a buffer to a new file path.

@item buf current
Print the name of the current buffer.

@item buf switch NAME
Switch the editor to display @var{NAME}.

@item buf info [NAME]
Print buffer metadata as key=value pairs:
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi buf info main.c
name=main.c
file=/home/zach/main.c
modified=no
size=4096
mode=c-mode
readonly=no
lines=128
@end example

@item buf rename OLD NEW
Rename a buffer.
@end table

@c ----- File Commands -------------------------------------------------
@section File Commands

@table @code
@item open PATH
Open a file in the editor.  Equivalent to @code{find-file}.

@item save [PATH]
Save the current buffer.  If @var{PATH} is given, save to that path
(like ``Save As'').

@item close [NAME]
Close a buffer.  If @var{NAME} is omitted, closes the current buffer.

@item recent [-n N]
List recently opened files, one per line.  Defaults to 20.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi recent -n 5
/home/zach/main.c
/home/zach/Makefile
/home/zach/README.md
/tmp/notes.txt
/etc/hosts
@end example
@end table

@c ----- Text Commands -------------------------------------------------
@section Text Commands

All text commands accept @option{-b BUF} to target a specific buffer
(defaults to current buffer).

@table @code
@item insert [-b BUF] [-p POS] TEXT
Insert @var{TEXT} at point, or at position @var{POS} if given.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi insert "hello world"
bacon$ cmacsgi insert -b scratch -p 1 "header text"
@end example

@item delete [-b BUF] START END
Delete the region between @var{START} and @var{END}.  Prints the
deleted text to stdout.

@item line [-b BUF] [N]
Print the Nth line of the buffer.  If @var{N} is omitted, prints the
current line.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi line 42
@end example

@item append [-b BUF] TEXT
Append @var{TEXT} to the end of the buffer.
@end table

@c ----- Navigation Commands -------------------------------------------
@section Navigation Commands

@table @code
@item point [-b BUF]
Print the cursor position as @code{LINE:COL}.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi point
42:12
@end example

@item goto [-b BUF] LINE[:COL]
Move the cursor to @var{LINE} and optionally @var{COL}.  Prints the
resulting position.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi goto 100
100:0
bacon$ cmacsgi goto 50:20
50:20
@end example
@end table

@c ----- Search Commands -----------------------------------------------
@section Search Commands

@table @code
@item search [-b BUF] [-r] [-c] PATTERN
Search for @var{PATTERN} in the buffer.  With @option{-r}, use regex.
With @option{-c}, print the match count instead of the first match.
Prints @code{LINE:COL:MATCH} for the first match.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi search -r "TODO.*fix"
12:4:TODO: fix this later

bacon$ cmacsgi search -c "printf"
7
@end example

@item replace [-b BUF] [-r] PATTERN REPLACEMENT
Replace all occurrences of @var{PATTERN} with @var{REPLACEMENT}.
With @option{-r}, treat @var{PATTERN} as a regex.  Prints the number
of replacements.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi replace "foo" "bar"
12

bacon$ cmacsgi replace -r "TODO\|FIXME" "DONE"
3
@end example

@item occur [-b BUF] PATTERN
List all lines matching @var{PATTERN} in grep-style format:
@code{LINENUM:LINE}.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi occur "#include"
1:#include <stdio.h>
2:#include <stdlib.h>
15:#include "myheader.h"
@end example
@end table

@c ----- Window Commands -----------------------------------------------
@section Window Commands

@table @code
@item win list
List windows.  Tab-separated: buffer name, width, height.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi win list
main.c	80	40
*compilation*	80	20
@end example

@item win split [-v]
Split the current window.  Default is horizontal (above/below).
@option{-v} splits vertically (side by side).

@item win close
Close the current window.

@item win only
Close all windows except the current one.

@item win next
Cycle to the next window.

@item win balance
Balance all window sizes equally.
@end table

@c ----- Configuration Commands ----------------------------------------
@section Configuration Commands

@table @code
@item set VAR VALUE
Set an Emacs variable.  Numbers and @code{t}/@code{nil} are passed
as-is; other values become strings.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi set fill-column 80
bacon$ cmacsgi set indent-tabs-mode nil
@end example

@item get VAR
Print the value of an Emacs variable.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi get fill-column
80
bacon$ cmacsgi get major-mode
c-mode
@end example

@item theme NAME
Load a color theme.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi theme modus-vivendi
@end example

@item font FAMILY [SIZE]
Set the default font.  @var{SIZE} is in points (converted to 1/10pt
internally).
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi font "JetBrains Mono" 14
@end example

@item mode NAME
Set the major mode.  Appends @code{-mode} automatically if needed.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi mode python
bacon$ cmacsgi mode org-mode
@end example
@end table

@c ----- Project Commands ----------------------------------------------
@section Project and Build Commands

@table @code
@item grep PATTERN [DIR]
Run a recursive grep.  Results appear in a CMacs compilation buffer.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi grep "TODO" src/
@end example

@item find FILENAME [DIR]
Find a file by name, searching from @var{DIR} or the current
directory.  Prints the path.

@item project-root
Print the project root directory (as determined by Emacs project.el).

@item compile CMD
Run a compilation command in CMacs.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi compile "make -j8"
@end example

@item next-error
Jump to the next compilation error.

@item prev-error
Jump to the previous compilation error.

@item diag [-b BUF]
List Flymake diagnostics in @code{FILE:LINE: SEVERITY: MESSAGE}
format.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi diag
main.c:42: warning: unused variable
main.c:87: error: expected semicolon
@end example
@end table

@c ----- Bookmark Commands ---------------------------------------------
@section Bookmark Commands

@table @code
@item mark set NAME
Set a bookmark at the current point.

@item mark list
List all bookmarks.  Tab-separated: name, file, position.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi mark list
debug-spot	/home/zach/main.c	1234
review-here	/home/zach/lib.c	567
@end example

@item mark jump NAME
Jump to a bookmark.

@item mark del NAME
Delete a bookmark.
@end table

@c ----- Clipboard Commands --------------------------------------------
@section Clipboard Commands

@table @code
@item copy [-b BUF] START END
Copy the region between @var{START} and @var{END} to the kill ring.

@item cut [-b BUF] START END
Cut the region (delete and copy to kill ring).

@item paste [-b BUF]
Paste from the kill ring at point.

@item clip list [-n N]
List the last @var{N} kill ring entries (default 10), one per line.
@end table

@c ----- Version Control Commands --------------------------------------
@section Version Control Commands

@table @code
@item vc status
Show git status (porcelain format).
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi vc status
## master...origin/master
 M src/main.c
?? newfile.txt
@end example

@item vc diff [FILE]
Show the diff for @var{FILE} or the entire working tree.

@item vc log [-n N] [FILE]
Show the git log (oneline format).  Defaults to 20 entries.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi vc log -n 5
abc1234 feat: add cmacsgi expansion
def5678 fix: buffer list formatting
@end example

@item vc blame [FILE]
Show git blame for @var{FILE} or the current buffer's file.
@end table

@c ----- Package Commands ----------------------------------------------
@section Package Commands

@table @code
@item pkg install NAME
Install an Emacs package.  Refreshes archives if needed.

@item pkg remove NAME
Remove an installed package.

@item pkg list [-a]
List installed packages.  With @option{-a}, list all available
packages.  Tab-separated: name, version, description.

@item pkg refresh
Refresh package archive contents.
@end table

@c ----- GI Commands ---------------------------------------------------
@section GObject Introspection Commands

@table @code
@item require NS [VERSION]
Load a GI namespace (typelib).
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi require GLib 2.0
@end example

@item call NS FUNC [ARGS...]
Call a GI function.  Arguments are auto-quoted for the Lisp reader:
numbers pass through, bare words become strings, quoted strings and
s-expressions pass through.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi call GLib get_user_name
"zach"
bacon$ cmacsgi call GLib compute_checksum_for_string 2 hello -1
"2cf24dba..."
@end example

@item list NS
List all functions in a namespace, one per line.
@end table

@c ----- Eval Command --------------------------------------------------
@section Eval Command

@table @code
@item eval EXPR
Evaluate an arbitrary elisp expression and print the result.  This is
the escape hatch for anything not covered by the other commands.
@example
bacon$ cmacsgi eval "(+ 40 2)"
42
bacon$ cmacsgi eval "(format-time-string \"%Y-%m-%d\")"
"2026-04-04"
@end example
@end table

@c ----- Other Original Commands ---------------------------------------
@section Other Commands

@table @code
@item find-file PATH
Open a file (alias for @code{open}).

@item message TEXT
Display a message in the CMacs minibuffer.
@end table

@c ----- Architecture --------------------------------------------------
@section Architecture

The @command{cmacsgi} builtin is a thin wrapper around the shared
@code{libcmacs-api} library (@pxref{CMacs API}).  The command
implementations, transport layer, and eval helpers all live in
@file{cmacs/api/}; the bacon module only contains the BaconCommand
GObject boilerplate and the dispatch table.

The primary IPC path (used by @code{M-x bacon}) is a Unix socketpair
with length-prefixed JSON messages:

@example
  CMacs (Emacs process)
       |
  cmacs-bacon-ipc  <--- socketpair fd ---> cmacs --bacon (child)
       |                                        |
  cmacs-eval-dispatch                     cmacsgi builtin
       |                                        |
  elisp eval / GI / file ops     libcmacs-api (FD transport backend)
@end example

@noindent
Wire protocol: @code{[4 bytes: uint32 BE length][length bytes: JSON]}.
Requests and responses are JSON objects with @code{id}, @code{method},
@code{params}/@code{result}/@code{error} fields.

The D-Bus fallback path (for external tools or standalone bacon) goes
through the session bus:

@example
  External tool / standalone bacon
       |
  GDBus proxy  --->  D-Bus session bus
       |                     |
       |              org.cmacs.Editor1
       |                     |
  CMacs (cmacs-eval-dispatch)
@end example

@noindent
Both paths share the same dispatch layer (@file{cmacs-eval-dispatch.c}),
so all methods behave identically regardless of transport.

Most subcommands build an elisp form on the client side and call the
@code{Eval} method.  The user never sees or writes elisp---the
@command{cmacsgi} command translates shell arguments into the
appropriate Lisp forms.

@section Environment Variables

@table @env
@item CMACS_IPC_FD
File descriptor number for the child side of the socketpair.  Set
automatically by @code{M-x bacon} before launching @command{cmacs
--bacon}.  When set, @command{cmacsgi} uses the fd transport (faster,
no bus daemon involved).

@item CMACS_DBUS_NAME
The D-Bus bus name of the running CMacs instance.  Set automatically
by @code{M-x bacon} as a fallback.  Example:
@code{org.cmacs.Editor.Pid12345}.  Used when @env{CMACS_IPC_FD} is
not available.

@item CMACS_MODULE_DIR
Directory containing @command{cmacsgi} module files.  Set by
@code{M-x bacon} so the child shell can locate loadable modules.
@end table

