@c ===================================================================
@node Crispy C Scripting
@chapter Crispy C Scripting

@cindex Crispy
@cindex C scripting

CMacs integrates the Crispy library for evaluating C code at runtime.
Scripts are compiled to shared objects, cached by content hash (SHA256),
and loaded into the Emacs process via @code{g_module_open()}.  Because
scripts run in-process, they can call back into Emacs through the
CMacs API (@pxref{CMacs API}).

@section Writing Config in C

Place an @file{init.c} in @file{~/.config/cmacs/} (or
@code{user-emacs-directory} as a fallback).  CMacs checks
@code{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cmacs/} first, then @file{~/.emacs.d/}, so
you can keep CMacs config separate from vanilla Emacs.  The file is
loaded after @file{init.el} via @code{crispy-run}.  The CMacs API
headers and library are automatically available---no manual
@code{CRISPY_PARAMS} needed.

@xref{CMacs API, , Example: init.c}, for a complete example.

@section Elisp Primitives

@table @code
@item crispy-eval CODE
Compile and execute inline C @var{CODE}.  Returns the exit code.
@item crispy-eval-string CODE
Execute C @var{CODE}, capturing stdout as a string.
@item crispy-run FILE &rest ARGS
Compile and execute a @file{.c} file with arguments.
@item crispy-compile FILE
Compile @var{FILE}, return the cached binary path.
@item crispy-repl-eval CODE
Evaluate in the persistent REPL.
@item crispy-repl-reset
Reset persistent REPL state.
@item crispy-cache-status
Return the crispy cache directory path.
@end table

@section Files

@table @file
@item cmacs/crispy/cmacs-crispy.c
C primitive implementation.  Registers the CMacs API direct dispatch
table so crispy scripts can call back into Emacs without IPC.
@item lisp/cmacs/cmacs-crispy.el
Elisp layer: REPL, minor mode, region/buffer evaluation.
@end table

@c ===================================================================
@node Build Configuration
@chapter Build Configuration

@section Configure Flags

CMacs features are off by default.  Enable them with:

@example
./configure \
  --with-cmacs-glib    \  # GLib event loop + GObject bridge + D-Bus
  --with-cmacs-gi      \  # GObject Introspection
  --with-cmacs-crispy  \  # Crispy C scripting
  --with-cmacs-bacon   \  # Bacon shell (bundled from deps/bacon/)
  --with-cmacs-gowl    \  # Gowl Wayland compositor (optional)
  --with-pgtk          \
  --with-native-compilation=aot
@end example

@noindent
@code{--with-cmacs-bacon} builds libbacon statically from
@file{deps/bacon/} and links it into the CMacs binary.  For distro
packaging, use @code{--with-cmacs-bacon=system} to link against a
system-installed @code{bacon-1.0} via pkg-config instead.

@section Dependencies

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .35 .4
@headitem Feature @tab pkg-config Module @tab Provides
@item cmacs-glib @tab glib-2.0 gobject-2.0 gio-2.0 gmodule-2.0
  @tab Event loop, GObject, D-Bus, GClosure
@item cmacs-gi @tab gobject-introspection-1.0 @tab GI bridge
@item cmacs-crispy @tab crispy @tab C scripting
@item cmacs-bacon (bundled) @tab glib-2.0 gio-2.0 gio-unix-2.0
  json-glib-1.0 yaml-0.1 readline @tab Shell (built from deps/bacon/)
@item cmacs-bacon (system) @tab bacon-1.0 @tab Shell (system package)
@end multitable

@section Building the Bacon Module

The @command{cmacsgi} module is compiled into the CMacs binary by
default (via the bundled libbacon static link).  For standalone or
development use, it can also be built as a loadable @file{.so}:

@example
cd cmacs/bacon/modules/
make
@end example

This produces @file{cmacs_gi.so} for use with an external
@command{bacon} binary.

@section Compile Flags

The Emacs build system picks up CMacs objects via @code{configure.ac}:

@example
CMACS_GLIB_OBJ="cmacs-glib-loop.o cmacs-gobject.o
                 cmacs-gclosure.o cmacs-dbus-service.o
                 cmacs-eval-dispatch.o"
CMACS_GI_OBJ="cmacs-gi.o"
CMACS_CRISPY_OBJ="cmacs-crispy.o"
CMACS_BACON_OBJ="cmacs-bacon.o cmacs-bacon-ipc.o"
@end example

C source files live under @file{cmacs/} and are symlinked into
@file{src/} for the build system.

@bye
